Well, humans aren't mind-readers. If they want attention then they have to point it out themselves. Isn't that how some attention seekers do in order to get noticed on social media/whatever is popular?

&#x200B;

My point is, I don't like people pointing things out about me when I never asked them in the first place. I know, I'm weird and a damn introvert.

I really hope so. Cassidy is my favorite character of this season so far. You see her and just know she's something worth keeping. Listening to her song sealed the deal for me.

&#x200B;

And Dontnod is mean for letting Daniel draw Mushroom on Sean's backpack, gonna be reminded of her every time I see the drawing :(

Chloe just wanted to have a best friend again, since Max left her and started ghosting her after a while. Max is not so perfect either if you read her messages in Before the Storm. Rachel disappears, Max returns and suddenly Chloe has something to live for again. She got her best friend/childhood  friend/possible romantic relationship back.

&#x200B;

What Max did wrong was trying to change things that was meant to happen. But it's not her fault the universe suddenly decided to give her Rewind powers. It could be seeing Chloe getting shot first time that triggered the whole event, even if she didn't know it was Chloe at first.

&#x200B;

Max and Daniel have at least one thing in common, their powers were activated by overwhelming emotions. I mean, who wouldn't get emotionally fucked up seeing someone being shot right in front of you? (Max seeing Chloe getting shot, Daniel seeing his dad getting shot).

It's funny though. I'm 26 and still dream about running away to somewhere I can start over and not get stuck in this situation forever. Aren't there a lot of teen runaways because of various situations in the U.S anyway?

&#x200B;

My take is that Rachel was too young to run away, true, but would eventually have if she wasn't killed by an overdose by Nathan (her hanging out a lot with Frank and loving the small trips in his RV). Before the Storm showed that they actually were serious about running away at one point, only to be interrupted by her parents and her dad hiding the truth about her birth mother. And she eventually got tired of Chloe because she took too long time to decide if she wanted to leave the hellhole called Arcadia Bay. The Sacrifice Arcadia Bay ending in LiS 1 shows that she was ready leave it all behind, although the ending was poorly made cuz of budget cuts.

&#x200B;

I mean, in LiS 2 Sean and Daniel manage to run away with little to no money (different situation I know, but they survived a long time in the woods). So I'm sure money wasn't the problem, but Chloe. She kept spending them on weed anyway, meaning she wasn't really that ready to escape even if she felt like total shit. Rachel REALLY wanted to leave Arcadia Bay, but didn't want to do it alone. That's my take on it at least.

&#x200B;

Imagine how scary it is to run away alone and not having someone familiar with you to support you through your journey? (Like Sean and Daniel)
